\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Unless otherwise noted, all experiments in this section use the v1 prompt (\S\ref{sec:model}; full templates in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_prompts}).

\subsection{Count-Degradation Curves}
\label{sec:count_curves}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_count_curves.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\hfill
\begin{minipage}[t]{0.48\columnwidth}
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{../../figures/paper/fig_bcs_scaling.pdf}
\end{minipage}
\caption{\textbf{(a)} Count-degradation curves across three IE tasks. NER recall generally declines and RE accuracy declines monotonically with schema size; MAVEN relaxed F1 increases (\S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}). \textbf{(b)} BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. The BCS--accuracy slope is consistent across the independent 16-relation pool and within-pool 16-relation subsets (permutation $p = 0.60$); the wider BCS range available at 80 relations produces larger absolute gaps.}
\label{fig:main_results}
\end{figure}

We first examine how extraction performance changes as the number of types in the prompt schema increases (Experiment~1; Figure~\ref{fig:main_results}a).

\paragraph{NER (Few-NERD).}
Recall generally declines from 0.72 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.42 at count${}\,=\,64$, a 42\% relative drop, with minor non-monotonicity at intermediate counts (e.g., 0.527 at count${}\,=\,22$ to 0.551 at count${}\,=\,44$; bootstrap 95\% CIs overlap: [0.484, 0.569] vs.\ [0.525, 0.570], indicating this reversal is within sampling variability). Precision moves in the opposite direction, rising from 0.11 to 0.52, reflecting a shift from over-prediction to under-prediction as more candidate types compete in the prompt. F1 exhibits an inverted-U shape, peaking at intermediate counts where the precision--recall trade-off is most balanced.

\paragraph{RE (FewRel).}
Accuracy drops monotonically from 85.7\% at count${}\,=\,1$ through 58.8\% at count${}\,=\,40$ to 50.4\% at count${}\,=\,80$, a near-halving when the model must discriminate among all 80 relation types simultaneously. (The count${}\,=\,40$ evaluation uses 40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool, not an independent schema.)

\paragraph{ED (MAVEN).}
In contrast to NER and RE, MAVEN relaxed F1 \emph{increases} from 0.106 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.247 at count${}\,=\,44$. This reversal arises from three properties of the MAVEN setup: (i)~trigger-level relaxed matching, which requires only token overlap rather than exact span match; (ii)~MAVEN's 168 event types derive from the FrameNet ontology, forming a same-domain hierarchy where co-presented types provide disambiguating context; and (iii)~extreme class imbalance---rare event types benefit from being presented alongside related types that prime the model toward the correct semantic region. We investigate this reversal further in \S\ref{sec:centrality_confound}.

\paragraph{Cross-model replication.}
Channel~1 replicates across model families: on Few-NERD, Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct shows a $-$43\,pp recall decline (0.702 at count${}\,=\,1$ to 0.273 at count${}\,=\,64$), compared to $-$34\,pp for Qwen3-32B (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model} in the Appendix). Per-batch recall correlation between the two models is $r = 0.725$, indicating that schema configuration drives difficulty rather than model-specific factors.

\paragraph{Summary.}
Schema size degrades extraction quality for NER and RE (Channel~1: count $\to$ recall decline), but this pattern does not hold universally. MAVEN's reversed trend demonstrates that task structure and evaluation protocol mediate the count--performance relationship. Both channels replicate on Llama-3.1-8B: the count channel ($r = 0.725$) and the confusability channel on RE (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}).

\subsection{Pairwise Confusability and Type Confusion}
\label{sec:pairwise}

We test whether pairwise SCS predicts type-level confusion rates. For each type pair $(t_i, t_j)$, we compute the confusion rate---the fraction of $t_i$ gold instances misclassified as $t_j$---and correlate it with $\text{SCS}(t_i, t_j)$. Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} reports Pearson correlations with bootstrap 95\% CIs.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{llrrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Dataset} & \textbf{Embed.} & \textbf{$n$ pairs} & \textbf{$r$} & \textbf{$r^2$} & \textbf{95\% CI} & \textbf{$p$} \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & Qwen3 & 1{,}953 & 0.112 & 0.013 & {[}0.076, 0.156{]} & ${<}10^{-12}$ \\
MAVEN & MiniLM & 11{,}026 & 0.129 & 0.017 & {[}0.104, 0.156{]} & ${<}10^{-10}$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Pairwise SCS--confusion correlation. $r^2$ indicates the fraction of variance in type confusion explained by embedding similarity. Both correlations are modest in magnitude but robust across datasets and embedding models ($p < 10^{-10}$).}
\label{tab:pairwise}
\end{table}

The correlations are statistically significant but modest: $r^2 \approx 1.3\text{--}1.7\%$, meaning pairwise confusability explains roughly 1.5\% of the variance in type confusion. Type-level cluster bootstrap, which accounts for the non-independence of pairs sharing a type, yields wider but still significant CIs (Few-NERD: [0.069, 0.219]; MAVEN: [0.081, 0.185]; $B = 10{,}000$); the pair-level CIs in Table~\ref{tab:pairwise} are tighter because they treat pairs as independent. We omit FewRel from the pairwise analysis: RE is a per-instance classification task where each sample maps to exactly one relation, so the directional confusion matrix is not directly comparable to the span-level confusion rates used for NER and ED. The BCS-controlled experiments (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}) provide stronger, interventional evidence for the confusability effect on RE.

Spearman rank correlations corroborate these findings. Across all type pairs, Qwen3 yields $\rho = 0.168$ ($p = 7.3 \times 10^{-14}$) and MiniLM yields $\rho = 0.144$ ($p = 1.7 \times 10^{-10}$). Restricting to pairs with nonzero confusion strengthens the association: $\rho = 0.272$ for Qwen3 and $\rho = 0.306$ for MiniLM. However, this increase warrants caution: conditioning on nonzero confusion selects for the dependent variable, which can inflate correlations. We report both estimates for transparency; the all-pairs correlations ($r = 0.112\text{--}0.129$) provide the unbiased baseline, while the nonzero-subset values ($\rho = 0.27\text{--}0.31$) characterize the signal among types the model actually confuses.

The remaining ${\sim}$98\% of variance in type confusion likely reflects multiple factors: entity and relation frequency in the evaluation corpus, prompt position effects \cite{lost-in-middle-2024}, mention-level ambiguity (e.g., ``Washington'' as person vs.\ location), context complexity, and type definition quality. We frame pairwise SCS as capturing one specific, actionable mechanism---semantic overlap between type labels is directly amenable to schema design and partitioning---rather than the totality of degradation. Although pairwise $r^2$ is modest, the signal compounds at the batch level into accuracy gaps reaching 31.2\,pp under extreme-batch comparison (\S\ref{sec:bcs_controlled}; typical-batch gap ${\approx}\,2\text{--}4$\,pp, \S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude}), demonstrating practical significance for schema optimization.

\subsection{BCS-Controlled Experiments}
\label{sec:bcs_controlled}

To move from correlational to interventional evidence, we fix the type count and vary BCS. Table~\ref{tab:bcs_controlled} reports FewRel accuracy under low-BCS and high-BCS batches at two schema sizes.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{lcrrrr}
\toprule
\textbf{Schema} & \textbf{Count} & \textbf{Low-BCS} & \textbf{High-BCS} & \textbf{$\Delta$ (pp)} & \textbf{95\% CI} \\
\midrule
16-rel & 8 & 88.9\% & 79.8\% & $-$9.1 & --- \\
80-rel & 8 & 82.3\% & 51.0\% & $-$31.2 & {[}17.7, 42.7{]} \\
80-rel & 16 & 82.8\% & 51.6\% & $-$31.2 & {[}22.9, 39.6{]} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{BCS-controlled FewRel accuracy. Low-BCS batches contain maximally dissimilar types; high-BCS batches contain maximally similar types. The 80-relation extreme-batch gap (31.2\,pp) reflects the wider BCS range; regression slopes are consistent across pools ($p = 0.60$; Table~\ref{tab:within_pool}; see \S\ref{sec:practical_magnitude} for typical-batch magnitudes). The 16-relation condition used a single extreme batch per BCS level; bootstrap CIs require multiple batches.}
\label{tab:bcs_controlled}
\end{table}

At the 16-relation scale, the extreme-batch gap is 9.1\,pp (single batch per level; Table~\ref{tab:bcs_controlled}); the quartile gap from the within-pool analysis is $-$5.31\,pp (CI: [$-$7.44, $-$3.19]; Table~\ref{tab:within_pool}). At 80 relations, the gap reaches 31.2\,pp (extreme-batch comparison; 95\% CI: [17.7, 42.7]). The effect is stable across count levels: at count${}\,=\,16$, the extreme-batch gap remains 31.2\,pp (CI: [22.9, 39.6]). An intermediate evaluation at count${}\,=\,40$ (40-relation subsets sampled from the 80-relation pool) yields accuracy 0.588 and BCS gaps of $-$33.3\,pp (count 8) and $-$31.8\,pp (count 16), consistent with the 80-relation results and confirming monotonic decline within the pool.

The larger absolute gap at 80 relations does not reflect a stronger per-unit sensitivity to confusability. As we show in the within-pool analysis below (\S\ref{sec:within_pool}), the BCS--accuracy slope is statistically indistinguishable across pools ($p = 0.60$). Rather, the 80-relation pool spans a wider BCS range (0.46--0.68 vs.\ 0.48--0.71 for 16-rel), enabling more extreme high-BCS batches that push accuracy further down the same gradient.

\paragraph{Within-pool controlled comparison.}
\label{sec:within_pool}
A potential confound in the cross-scale comparison is that the 16-relation and 80-relation pools contain different types, so the observed gap could reflect pool composition rather than confusability. To control for this, we draw 100 random 16-relation subsets from the 80-relation pool and evaluate under the same BCS-controlled protocol (Table~\ref{tab:within_pool}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
