\paragraph{Practical magnitude.}
\label{sec:practical_magnitude}
For representative batches drawn from the 80-relation pool ($n{=}100$ random 16-type subsets; Table~\ref{tab:within_pool}), the typical BCS span (IQR = 0.054) yields an expected accuracy gap of 2.2\,pp (95\% CI: [0.4, 4.0]), roughly 7\% of the 31.2\,pp extreme-batch gap. A direct quartile comparison confirms this: the mean accuracy of bottom-quartile BCS batches is 82.8\%, versus 78.9\% for top-quartile batches, a gap of 3.9\,pp. The 31.2\,pp figure reflects the maximum BCS contrast available in the pool (extreme-batch comparison), not the typical batch experience; the 10th--90th percentile BCS span produces a gap of 4.2\,pp.

\paragraph{Cross-model validation.}
The confusability effect replicates on Meta-Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct: on FewRel, the BCS gap is $-$27.1\,pp at count${}\,=\,8$ (low-BCS 78.1\% vs.\ high-BCS 51.0\%) and $-$23.9\,pp at count${}\,=\,16$ (75.5\% vs.\ 51.6\%), reaching 77--87\% of Qwen3-32B's $-$31.2\,pp extreme-batch gap. Strikingly, high-BCS accuracy is consistent across tested models (Qwen3-32B and Llama-3.1-8B): both models converge to $\approx$51\% under high confusability (count${}\,=\,8$: 51.0\% vs.\ 51.0\%; count${}\,=\,16$: 51.6\% vs.\ 51.6\%), while cross-model divergence is confined to the low-BCS regime where model capacity matters. This pattern indicates that confusable type sets create a task-inherent difficulty floor consistent across the tested models.

\subsection{Multi-Channel Degradation}
\label{sec:multi_channel}

We decompose extraction errors into an error taxonomy (full data in Table~\ref{tab:error_taxonomy}, Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_figures}; see also Figure~\ref{fig:error_taxonomy} in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_figures}). Intra-batch substitution (IntraSub) scales monotonically with BCS in both tasks: $\rho = 1.0$ ($p < 0.001$, $n=148$ batches) for NER and $\rho = 0.954$ ($p < 10^{-6}$, $n=154$ batches) for RE. Critically, 100\% of type confusion errors in RE are intra-batch---the model exclusively confuses types present in the current prompt. The tasks diverge on missed-entity rates: RE rates remain flat (10--16\%), but NER rates are substantially higher (43--65\%) and non-monotonic, suggesting that confusable type sets impair entity detection itself in span-level extraction. Extreme-BCS batches produce $9.9\text{--}27.5\times$ more confusion than low-BCS batches, confirming that BCS captures a functionally relevant dimension of schema difficulty.

These results establish two degradation channels: \textbf{Channel~1} (count $\to$ recall decline, \S\ref{sec:count_curves}) and \textbf{Channel~2} (confusability $\to$ type confusion). In RE, the channels are cleanly separable---confusability drives substitution errors while detection remains stable. In NER, Channel~2 additionally impairs entity detection, suggesting that for span-level extraction, type ambiguity and boundary ambiguity are coupled.

\subsection{Centrality Confound on Homogeneous Schemas}
\label{sec:centrality_confound}

Per-type analysis on MAVEN reveals an anomalous \emph{positive} BCS--error correlation (Qwen3 $\rho = +0.184$, $p = 0.025$; MiniLM $\rho = +0.208$, $p = 0.011$), opposite to the confusability hypothesis. This traces to same-domain embedding collapse \cite{cai-isotropy-clusters-2021}: MAVEN's FrameNet-derived types cluster tightly in representation space, so a type's mean BCS conflates confusability with centrality---high-BCS types are also well-represented types that the model extracts reliably. Few-NERD's cross-domain schema does not exhibit this confound ($\rho = -0.293$, $p = 0.02$, $n = 63$), as its types span multiple embedding clusters. Critically, the pairwise SCS signal remains intact on MAVEN ($r = 0.129$, $p < 10^{-10}$; Table~\ref{tab:pairwise}); the problem lies in per-type aggregation, not the underlying pairwise relationship. A residualized analysis controlling for log frequency and degree centrality confirms the positive correlation vanishes ($p > 0.2$; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_residualized_bcs}). Mean BCS is thus a reliable schema difficulty predictor only for cross-domain schemas; same-domain schemas require pairwise SCS or explicit centrality adjustment.

\begin{abstract}
Training-free LLM information extraction---prompting models with target schemas without fine-tuning---degrades sharply as schema complexity grows: NER recall drops from 0.72 to 0.42 with 64 candidate types, and RE accuracy falls from 85.7\% to 50.4\% with 80 relations. Prior work conflates type count with type confusability, leaving the mechanisms unclear. We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating these factors across three IE tasks and three datasets, introducing the Schema Confusability Score (SCS) and two experimental paradigms: count curves that vary schema size while randomizing confusability, and BCS-controlled experiments that fix size while varying confusability. Our experiments reveal two degradation channels: a count channel that generally suppresses recall as candidate types increase, and a confusability channel where semantically similar types trigger intra-batch substitution errors---cleanly separable in RE; in NER, confusability additionally impairs entity detection. The BCS--accuracy relationship is robust across schema sizes: the regression slope is consistent between independent 16-type and within-pool 80-type evaluations (permutation $p = 0.60$), with the quartile gap reaching $-$5.3\,pp (95\% CI: [$-$7.4, $-$3.2]) on the independent 16-relation pool. On MAVEN's same-domain schema (168 event types), per-type confusability reverses direction due to a centrality confound, though pairwise confusability remains significant. Both channels replicate on Llama-3.1-8B (count $r = 0.725$; confusability $\Delta = -27$\,pp), with high-BCS accuracy converging to a floor consistent across both tested models. Our confusability-aware decomposition yields an informative negative result: merge amplification destroys precision across all strategies, identifying a structural challenge for union-based merge pipelines in zero-shot IE.
\end{abstract}

